We introduce high-efficiency glass interposers between multicore fibers (MCFs) and photonic integrated circuits (PICs) that can be assembled without active alignment. We show that the same hardware stack can be used for both detection of core positions inside a MCF via multiphoton (MP) fluorescence imaging and for direct laser writing (DLW) of waveguides in glass. MP images of the fiber cores in MCFs are presented as well as high-throughput DLW glass interposers for coupling MCF to linear arrays with a variety of geometries and mode shapes. Combining the two techniques can enable the writing of MCF-PIC interposers for on-off chip coupling at high efficiency without the need for active alignment.